#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ea8cba7-d03a-516f-a0c3-30fb8354dfdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569622} {:answer/id #uuid "86253246-2caa-5e0b-abae-547f561590f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569664} {:answer/id #uuid "98428f30-5194-58f2-b856-5fc56747aac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569687} {:answer/id #uuid "d6757daa-1d29-5c10-947c-afd176f4f5d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569779} {:answer/id #uuid "d952e124-7588-56a7-81b3-154bceef8a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d952e124-7588-56a7-81b3-154bceef8a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569785} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc076be1-fa1d-5a8e-9f7b-f0d944a99952" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2c0ab81-ce73-5f44-b936-e8bb7ee9594f" :question/math-academy-id "q-100794" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x}=11x\\cdot {5}^{x}+4\\cdot {5}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x} & =11x\\cdot {5}^{x}+4\\cdot {5}^{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x}-11x\\cdot {5}^{x}-4\\cdot {5}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-11x-4)\\cdot {5}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-11x-4=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $3{x}^{2}-11x-4=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+1)(x-4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{1}{3},4.$\n\n- The equation ${5}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{1}{3},4,$ and their product is $(-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 4=-\\frac{4}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c7135a8-799d-5ef5-9d3d-470881e76d26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569548} {:answer/id #uuid "adcaa9ff-45f7-5db6-b2e3-d82e71396783" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569716} {:answer/id #uuid "beb695a1-1361-556b-b4fb-bedb79dc1f93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569748} {:answer/id #uuid "d2eb82f2-a647-5584-afd7-3c3b5045b584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569775} {:answer/id #uuid "ea325e7e-7c0b-5864-bded-5700ce421acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea325e7e-7c0b-5864-bded-5700ce421acf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569815} :answer-field/id #uuid "459604d3-c555-5e7f-b4d3-0ad596a161eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3499241b-bbd1-5943-8b7b-1fe387959bfa" :question/math-academy-id "q-100799" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $9{x}^{2}{e}^{x}=36{e}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}{e}^{x} & =36{e}^{x} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}{e}^{x}-36{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (9{x}^{2}-36){e}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}-36=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{e}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $9{x}^{2}-36=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+6)(3x-6)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-2,2.$\n\n- The equation ${e}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${e}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-2,2,$ and their product is $(-2)\\cdot 2=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54b95013-9673-571d-a385-1ada2dbd7596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54b95013-9673-571d-a385-1ada2dbd7596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290994} :answer-field/id #uuid "d77c3cd4-4822-5b88-b531-15ff6b1e643c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292391} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abb9bd8e-4e36-5095-84b1-7e4d92640618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abb9bd8e-4e36-5095-84b1-7e4d92640618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291378} :answer-field/id #uuid "e319a330-863d-5a2b-83b4-4b5600e073c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77ac52fb-b13e-5bb0-9619-5d3b00902a0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-264778" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x+3}-7x\\cdot {2}^{x+3}+3\\cdot {2}^{x+3}=0$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x+3}-7x\\cdot {2}^{x+3}+3\\cdot {2}^{x+3} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-7x+3){2}^{x+3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-7x+3=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{2}^{x+3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-7x+3=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x-1)(x-3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{2},3.$\n\n- The equation ${2}^{x+3}=0$ has no solutions because ${2}^{x+3}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157438 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e00ad2a-0346-5764-8bd0-cbcc0f4a797a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e00ad2a-0346-5764-8bd0-cbcc0f4a797a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291167} :answer-field/id #uuid "4304fe74-f9d5-5270-981d-8bec5b17751e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291954} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c61ad1b3-442b-57c6-94cb-530214be2c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c61ad1b3-442b-57c6-94cb-530214be2c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291485} :answer-field/id #uuid "db84ff3f-88d9-57d9-80ab-3958548a8d86" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292416}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4930566b-e427-5630-b850-4beaebc0bb52" :question/math-academy-id "q-264787" :question/problem "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $4{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-4}=144\\cdot {5}^{x-4}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-4} & =144\\cdot {5}^{x-4} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-4}-144\\cdot {5}^{x-4} & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-144)\\cdot {5}^{x-4} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}-144=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{x-4}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $4{x}^{2}-144=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}-36) & =0 \\\\ 4(x+6)(x-6) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-6,6.$\n\n- The equation $4=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${5}^{x-4}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{x-4}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-6\\,\\text{and}\\,x=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44378ce2-6b6d-50f9-8219-f673e5079ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569538} {:answer/id #uuid "605ed3c1-ae12-54a3-81f7-60de52284930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569591} {:answer/id #uuid "61b10d9f-a72c-52f5-9148-9af198ae4115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569595} {:answer/id #uuid "660eda51-d232-5b88-9bd1-a2c03b96321a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569601} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9d3203-fb35-582c-af0f-5edb1642fe68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "660eda51-d232-5b88-9bd1-a2c03b96321a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569601} :answer-field/id #uuid "7de9c953-9396-5e7b-9be9-017863373246" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12b67edd-e409-5ea0-9859-9fa94b363ebb" :question/math-academy-id "q-88457" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}{e}^{3x}-4x{e}^{3x}-12{e}^{3x}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{3x}-4x{e}^{3x}-12{e}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4x-12){e}^{3x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-4x-12=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{e}^{3x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-4x-12=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+2)(x-6)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-2,6.$\n\n- The equation ${e}^{3x}=0$ has no solutions because ${e}^{3x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-2,6,$ and their product is $(-2)\\cdot 6=-12.$"}]}
